00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:01.419 align:start position:0%
 
[Applause]  hundreds of protesters shouted no police  no curfew as they march through the  capital Mogadishu on Saturday they say  the shooting of a civilian by a police  officer and forcing a curfew on Friday  night is another example of violence and  abuse by the security forces which they  say happens too often this demonstration  will continue until the government  senior officials listen to the outcry of  the population we want the government to  reverse the curfew decisions that is  imposed on the residents during this  holy month the shooting happened near  the headquarters of the Somali National  Intelligence and Security Agency  Somalia's police chief fired the  commissioner in charge of the area on  Saturday and the mayor of Mogadishu  apologized for the shooting first I  respect that the youth of Mogadishu need  to express their anger for the killing  of a civilian they have the right to do  so secondly I'm very sorry for us and I  share the same sorrow you are feeling  Somalia has poor health care facilities  and a limited capacity to trace and test  those infected with the corona virus the  government introduced a lockdown more  than two weeks ago as the number of  people infected with kovat 19:00  increased but there has to be trust  between the public and the police for it  to work effectively and that's in short  supply victoria Gaytan b al jazeera

